@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Recursive CTE";
}

<div class="container-fluid px-4 py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            Recursive CTE
        </h1>

        <p class="text-muted">
            Learn how Recursive Common Table Expressions work in SQL Server
            and how they can be used with hierarchical data.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         WHAT IS RECURSIVE CTE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                What is a Recursive CTE?
            </h2>

            <p>
                A <strong>Recursive CTE</strong> is a Common Table Expression
                that refers to itself.
            </p>

            <p>
                Recursive CTEs are useful when data has a hierarchical
                or parent-child relationship.
            </p>

            <p class="mb-0">
                Common examples include employee-manager relationships,
                organization structures, category hierarchies and
                folder structures.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         BASIC STRUCTURE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Basic Structure of Recursive CTE
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>WITH CTE_Name AS
(
    -- Anchor Member

    SELECT ...

    UNION ALL

    -- Recursive Member

    SELECT ...
    FROM CTE_Name
    ...
)
SELECT *
FROM CTE_Name;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         TWO PARTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Two Important Parts
            </h2>

            <h4 class="fw-bold">
                1. Anchor Member
            </h4>

            <p>
                The anchor member is the initial query that provides
                the starting rows for the recursive operation.
            </p>

            <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4">
                2. Recursive Member
            </h4>

            <p class="mb-0">
                The recursive member refers back to the CTE and retrieves
                the next level of related records.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EMPLOYEE TABLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example: Employee Hierarchy
            </h2>

            <p>
                Consider an Employees table containing employees and
                their managers.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>EmployeeId | EmployeeName | ManagerId
-----------------------------------------
1          | Amit          | NULL
2          | Rahul         | 1
3          | Priya         | 1
4          | Sneha         | 2
5          | Raj           | 2
6          | Neha          | 3</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Here, <strong>ManagerId</strong> represents the manager
                of an employee.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         RECURSIVE CTE EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Recursive CTE Example
            </h2>

            <p>
                The following query starts with the top-level employee
                and then finds employees reporting to that employee.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>WITH EmployeeHierarchy AS
(
    -- Anchor Member
    SELECT
        EmployeeId,
        EmployeeName,
        ManagerId,
        0 AS Level
    FROM Employees
    WHERE ManagerId IS NULL

    UNION ALL

    -- Recursive Member
    SELECT
        E.EmployeeId,
        E.EmployeeName,
        E.ManagerId,
        EH.Level + 1
    FROM Employees E
    INNER JOIN EmployeeHierarchy EH
        ON E.ManagerId = EH.EmployeeId
)
SELECT
    EmployeeId,
    EmployeeName,
    ManagerId,
    Level
FROM EmployeeHierarchy
ORDER BY Level, EmployeeId;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         RESULT
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Result
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>EmployeeId</th>
                            <th>EmployeeName</th>
                            <th>ManagerId</th>
                            <th>Level</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td>1</td>
                            <td>Amit</td>
                            <td>NULL</td>
                            <td>0</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>2</td>
                            <td>Rahul</td>
                            <td>1</td>
                            <td>1</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>3</td>
                            <td>Priya</td>
                            <td>1</td>
                            <td>1</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>4</td>
                            <td>Sneha</td>
                            <td>2</td>
                            <td>2</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>5</td>
                            <td>Raj</td>
                            <td>2</td>
                            <td>2</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>6</td>
                            <td>Neha</td>
                            <td>3</td>
                            <td>2</td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         HOW RECURSION WORKS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                How Recursive CTE Works
            </h2>

            <ol>

                <li class="mb-3">
                    The anchor member identifies the starting records.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-3">
                    The recursive member finds records related to the
                    previous level.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-3">
                    The process continues until no more matching records
                    are found.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-3">
                    Each recursion can represent another level in the
                    hierarchy.
                </li>

                <li>
                    The final query returns all generated rows.
                </li>

            </ol>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         LEVEL COLUMN
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Understanding the Level Column
            </h2>

            <p>
                A level column can be added to a recursive CTE to
                identify the depth of each record in the hierarchy.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>0 = Top Level
1 = First Child Level
2 = Second Child Level
3 = Third Child Level
...</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         UNION ALL
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Why UNION ALL is Used?
            </h2>

            <p>
                Recursive CTEs commonly use
                <strong>UNION ALL</strong> to combine the anchor member
                and recursive member.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>WITH EmployeeHierarchy AS
(
    SELECT ...
    
    UNION ALL
    
    SELECT ...
    FROM EmployeeHierarchy
)
SELECT *
FROM EmployeeHierarchy;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         MAXRECURSION
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                MAXRECURSION
            </h2>

            <p>
                SQL Server provides the
                <strong>MAXRECURSION</strong> query option to control
                the maximum number of recursion levels.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>WITH EmployeeHierarchy AS
(
    SELECT
        EmployeeId,
        EmployeeName,
        ManagerId,
        0 AS Level
    FROM Employees
    WHERE ManagerId IS NULL

    UNION ALL

    SELECT
        E.EmployeeId,
        E.EmployeeName,
        E.ManagerId,
        EH.Level + 1
    FROM Employees E
    INNER JOIN EmployeeHierarchy EH
        ON E.ManagerId = EH.EmployeeId
)
SELECT *
FROM EmployeeHierarchy
OPTION (MAXRECURSION 100);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         USE CASES
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Common Use Cases
            </h2>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Employee-manager hierarchies.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Organization structures.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Product category hierarchies.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Folder and directory structures.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Parent-child relationships.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         ADVANTAGES
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Advantages of Recursive CTE
            </h2>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Useful for hierarchical data.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Avoids writing multiple queries for different levels.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Makes hierarchical queries easier to understand.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Can dynamically process multiple hierarchy levels.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Useful for parent-child relationships.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-warning shadow-sm mb-4">

        <h4 class="fw-bold">
            Important Points
        </h4>

        <ul class="mb-0">

            <li class="mb-2">
                A recursive CTE refers to itself.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                It contains an anchor member and a recursive member.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                UNION ALL is commonly used between the two members.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Recursive CTEs are useful for hierarchical data.
            </li>

            <li>
                MAXRECURSION can be used to control recursion depth.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-5">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p>
                A Recursive CTE is a CTE that refers to itself. It consists
                of an anchor member that provides the starting records and
                a recursive member that retrieves related records.
            </p>

            <p class="mb-0">
                Recursive CTEs are especially useful for employee
                hierarchies, organization structures, categories,
                folders and other parent-child relationships.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>



